# {{NAME}} Workspace

Parent folder that holds these repos side-by-side and auto-wires their AI coding assistant configs (agents, commands, skills, hooks) into one session. Works with Claude Code and Codex.

{{REPO_LIST}}

For team members who regularly work across all of them in a single AI coding assistant session.

## Setup

```sh
{{CLONE_INSTRUCTION}}
cd {{NAME}}
./bootstrap.sh
claude    # or: codex
```

`bootstrap.sh` will:
1. Clone any missing sub-repos listed in `workspace.conf`.
2. Create any declared worktrees.
3. Prune dead symlinks under `.claude/` and `.codex/`.
4. Symlink every `<repo>/.claude/{agents,commands,skills,hooks}/*` into this workspace's `.claude/`.
5. Symlink every `<repo>/.codex/skills/*` into this workspace's `.codex/skills/`.

It's idempotent — rerun any time.

## Daily use

- `./pull-all.sh` — fast-forward pull every sub-repo and worktree.
- `./bootstrap.sh` — rerun whenever a sub-repo adds / renames / removes an agent, command, skill, or hook.
- `./worktree-status.sh` — show branch, dirty state, and ahead/behind for all repos and worktrees.

## Multi-repo feature workflow (worktrees)

When you need to work on a feature that spans multiple repos simultaneously:

```sh
# Create worktrees for the feature branch (all repos or specific ones)
./worktree-create.sh feature/payments
./worktree-create.sh feature/payments web-app api   # specific repos only

# Wire up .claude/ and .codex/ symlinks for the new worktrees
./bootstrap.sh

# Check status across everything
./worktree-status.sh

# Work on the feature — each worktree is at <repo>--<branch>/
# Commit and push in each independently, open PRs per repo

# When done, clean up
./worktree-remove.sh feature/payments
./bootstrap.sh
```

Worktrees share the git object store with their source repo — they are lightweight (no full re-clone) and each tracks its own branch independently.

### Adding repos to an existing feature branch

If you discover mid-feature that another repo needs changes, add it without disrupting existing worktrees:

```sh
./worktree-create.sh feature/payments shared-lib
./bootstrap.sh
```

The script skips repos that already have worktrees for that branch — only the new repo gets one.

### Worktree status

`./worktree-status.sh` shows a table with directory, type (clone/wt), branch, and status (clean/dirty + ahead/behind remote):

```
DIRECTORY                      TYPE     BRANCH                    STATUS
---------                      ----     ------                    ------
web-app                        clone    main                      clean
web-app--feature-payments      wt       feature/payments          dirty (2 files) [+3/-0]
```

### Worktree removal safety

`./worktree-remove.sh` refuses to remove worktrees with uncommitted changes. Commit or stash first, then retry. After removal, it prints commands to optionally delete the local branches.

## Adding a new agent (or command / skill / hook)

1. Create the file inside the relevant sub-repo: `<sub-repo>/.claude/agents/foo.md` or `<sub-repo>/.codex/skills/foo/SKILL.md`.
2. Commit and push it inside that sub-repo.
3. Each teammate runs `./bootstrap.sh` to pick it up. Done.

## Adding a new sub-repo to the workspace

1. Edit `workspace.conf` and append `"name|git-url"` to the `REPOS=(...)` array.
2. Add the repo's directory name to `.gitignore`.
3. (Optional) add it to `{{NAME}}.code-workspace` for a multi-root view in VS Code / Cursor.
4. Run `./bootstrap.sh`.

## Why a workspace shell?

The sub-repos are gitignored here — this shell **never** tracks their internal changes. It only owns the cross-repo glue: `bootstrap.sh`, `pull-all.sh`, `worktree-*.sh`, `workspace.conf`, the workspace `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md`, and the symlink layout.

Coworkers who don't need the cross-repo setup can ignore this shell entirely and just work inside the individual sub-repos directly — agents are committed to those repos and load natively.

